.TH PENDTRIAL 1
.SH NAME
pendtrial \- does keeping a half-read frame deliver more Styx?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/radio/pendtrial.py
.I sat-session
.I gnd-session
.RI [ pairs ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The cc1120 driver pops a length byte off a frame whose payload may still
be in the air and cannot push it back.  It used to flush the FIFO there;
it now keeps the length and finishes the frame when the rest turns up.
.B "One run of each is not an answer:"
.IR armv7mstyx 's
.I bigread
either completes or times out, so a run is a coin, and three heads prove
nothing about a coin that was already landing heads sometimes.
.PP
This alternates the two arms on
.B "one kernel."
The arm is a line in
.IR radiocal (1)'s
.I /lib/radio
\-
.B pend=0
or nothing \- which the station writes to
.B #W/ctl
at boot, so the only difference between arms is that line.
.B "A run of the old behaviour from an OLD KERNEL compares every other difference between the two builds at the same time,"
which is how this bench has produced three confident wrong findings.
.PP
.I Pairs
defaults to 3.  Each pair is one
.B pend=1
run and one
.B pend=0
run, each preceded by a build and two flashes, and each gets a line:
.DS
.CW "1 pend=1 both boards say pend=1    bigread OK   2 reads | rxstall 2 ..."
.DE
.B $PENDTRIALDIR
is where the full output of each run is kept, one file per row, so the
table can be re-scored without re-running the bench.
.SH "WHY IT ALTERNATES, AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO CONCLUDE"
.B "Alternating rather than blocking, because the bench drifts."
The boards warm up, the room changes, and six runs of one arm followed
by six of the other attributes the afternoon to the change.
.PP
.B "Both boards have to print the same arm."
Each station prints its pend setting at boot and the arm is a file in
each board's image: one board taking it and the other not is a
half-armed run, and scoring that as a clean arm is how a trial reports a
difference that was never applied.  A row that does not have two
agreeing prints says
.B "(NOT BOTH)"
and is left out of the totals.
.PP
.B "And it says which rows could have shown anything."
The fault this change was written for is an
.I edge-driven
partial: the drain loop looked at a FIFO mid-packet and the old code
flushed the frame, which is the population the original failure came
from \-
.B "rxstall 0 rxpartial 1" .
A partial found by the once-a-second
.I stall
detector is a different animal, a truncated transmission frozen in the
FIFO, and
.B "both arms handle it."
So a run whose only partials came from the stall detector did not meet
the fault, and
.B "a table that reads three-for-three against three-for-three is inconclusive."
The harness prints that it is, rather than leaving the reader to notice.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/radio
.TP
.B /lib/radio
the
.B pend
key, per board
.TP
.B tools/f407/flashverify.sh
the build and the proof that the board runs what was flashed
.TP
.B tools/radio/twostyx.py
one run of both ends
.SH SOURCE
.B tools/radio/pendtrial.py
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR radio (3),
.IR radiocal (1),
.B doc/radio-plan.ms
.SH BUGS
It takes about four minutes a row, almost all of it flashing, and it
reflashes even when only
.I /lib/radio
changed, because the root is inside the image.
